<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aberffraw</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aberffraw is a village on the south-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> that served as the chief royal seat of the kings and princes of Gwynedd in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a> used the title 'Prince of Aberffraw and Lord of Snowdon' from about 1230. Little of the royal court survives; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> were taken for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle">Beaumaris Castle</a> in the early 14th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aberffraw.jpg" alt="Aberffraw" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Heaton · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aberffraw_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3923739.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Aberffraw is a village and community on the south-west coast of the Isle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, on the west bank of the River Ffraw, nine miles from the county town Llangefni. Its name is often shortened to Berffro in local Welsh. It is one of the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, about 9,000 years old. At Trwyn Du in Aberffraw Bay a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> kerb cairn of about 2000 BCE was excavated in 1977 and found to stand on a deposit of 7,000 flint tools and two axes from about 7000 BCE. Nearby is Din Dryfol, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> chambered tomb of about 3000 BCE, and the remains of stone hut circles and roundhouses lived in from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> until the Roman period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Welsh mythology Aberffraw is the setting of the wedding feast of Branwen and Matholwch. The court is said to have been founded in the 5th century by Cadwallon Lawhir ap Einion. Cadwallon ap Cadfan moved the royal court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a> in the 7th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a> rebuilt the residence in 873 and returned the capital of Gwynedd to the Aberffraw cantref. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarawd-ap-rhodri">Anarawd ap Rhodri</a> founded the Royal House of Aberffraw, whose members ruled as kings of Gwynedd and were eventually titled princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Llywelyn ab Iorwerth re-established the palace as a residence in 1201.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> between 852 and 919 and again between 950 and 998, including a raid on Aberffraw in 968, and King Maredudd ab Owain paid the ransom for the Welsh they had enslaved. The court was dismantled in 1317 for building materials. On 6 December 1331 a sandstorm buried 186 acres between Aberffraw and Rhosneigr and drove families south to Llanddwyn and Newborough. Around 1500 the Meyrick family of Bodorgan received the Crown lease of the cantref's manor lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The community had 597 residents at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and 67.5 per cent of the population could speak Welsh in 2011. Near the village are the Barclodiad y Gawres Neolithic burial chamber and part of the Anglesey Coastal Path. St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-beuno">Beuno</a>'s Church in the village dates from the 12th century and is Grade II* listed, and the 12th-century St Cwyfan's Church stands on the tidal island of Cribinau. The Aberffraw dunes, 357 hectares, are a site of special scientific interest and part of a Special Area of Conservation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aberffraw” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
